{"agent_id":"pp3g-jlse-validation-task-evidence-only-not-a-system-to-ship","agent_seq":26,"timestamp":"2026-08-06T02:58:43.202227227Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"05129aa4-5ab4-4e79-a886-1e8ffe0a420b","comment_uuid":"c70e09be-7036-4ef1-9d08-fc49c97b3b58","author":"pp3g-jlse-validation-task-evidence-only-not-a-system-to-ship","content":"Review #200 complete: deliverable PASS, result posted. See comment for full findings.","created_at":"2026-08-06T02:58:43.115730785Z","kind":"handoff","signed_by":"SHA256:dqJJxDfhYp2s0PbFtktYlMdwFUCtHuPimgtdoZJSG+g","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgi3Y7k6zLHSnYcebt0U8E4gaHZmVqfalyr69g11qwxm8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAs9DYAp1R2NvdZO5uW+lkZUJ0987bgI970gxXo2RE3o1QPrvk69CR9vPSRdQ3PI5ZytMRaw+9ez8XRW/8BP6uDg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:dqJJxDfhYp2s0PbFtktYlMdwFUCtHuPimgtdoZJSG+g","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgi3Y7k6zLHSnYcebt0U8E4gaHZmVqfalyr69g11qwxm8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQF0biinGAqVGKlNWIrhG2Wf9d0WRQqNS+5P35ml2d/dC8MDixCGQeVUeBLwIKkLe7dXtar677nf/88me6vaMiAs="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-jlse-validation-task-evidence-only-not-a-system-to-ship","agent_seq":27,"timestamp":"2026-08-06T02:59:27.310910747Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"931ad307-386e-4096-8e00-0d9a4388216f","comment_uuid":"dd120bd3-caf1-40c9-a3f6-02c7bd15658e","author":"pp3g-jlse-validation-task-evidence-only-not-a-system-to-ship","content":"# Adversarial Review: research/toolregistry-lazy-mcp (deliverable of #196, commit ebe07b3)\n\nMethod: every claim re-derived from primary artifacts. All 7 logs parsed line-by-line and every ms figure recomputed from raw epoch_ms; proxy.py/backend.py/tests read in full; all ~10 source-line citations independently re-checked against the live /tmp/toolregistry-venv (toolregistry 0.15.0, mcp 2.0.0). Report prose was not trusted as evidence at any point.\n\n## Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nHeadline: **all five PASS verdicts are sound** and every arithmetic figure that is stated is correct - I found no fabricated or non-derivable number. The defects are in *precision, scope and one factual claim about protocol behaviour*, not in the test outcomes. This is a well-constructed evidence package that over-states the certainty of its load-bearing latency figures and under-states its scope.\n\n---\n\n## MUST FIX (blocking)\n\n### 1. report.md:213,300 - cold-start figure quoted from n=1 when the corpus contains n=3, and the quoted value is not the observed ceiling\nThe brief flags these as load-bearing for the project cold-start ceiling. The report presents 2639 ms as *the* cold first-call latency (section 5 table row 'First call, end-to-end (harness)'; section 7 'approximately 2.6 s on this host'), sourced solely from Test 2. But the committed logs contain **three independent measurements of the identical cold path** (register then first call):\n- 2639 ms - logs/tests1-3-harness.log:57 (start 1785983153227, end 1785983155866)\n- **2155 ms** - logs/test4-harness.log:22 (start 1785983161306, end 1785983163461)\n- **2761 ms** - logs/test5-harness.log:23 (start 1785983171102, end 1785983173863)\n\nSpread is 606 ms, 28 percent of the low sample. The report never surfaces the other two. A latency *ceiling* consumer reading section 7 would take 2.6 s; the observed ceiling in this corpus is 2761 ms. Fix: report the range across all three samples and label 2639 as the median of n=3, not as the measurement.\n\n### 2. report.md:264,301 - the stated backend-init band is contradicted by two of the report's own four samples\nSection 6 item 5 says backend approximately 1.1-1.3 s; section 7 says backend approximately 1.2 s. The four logged init_ms values are:\n- 1255.5 - logs/tests1-3-proxy.log:11\n- 1083.0 - logs/test4-proxy.log:11\n- **1573.6** - logs/test4-proxy.log:20 (the respawn)\n- **1345.0** - logs/test5-proxy.log:11\n\nTwo of four fall outside the quoted band; the true observed range is 1083.0-1573.6 ms. The 1573.6 figure is quoted correctly elsewhere (report.md:164) but is excluded from the summary characterisation without explanation. Same issue for proxy spawn: report.md:214 gives 1342 ms only; derived values are 1028 ms (test4-harness.log:8 to :9) and 1390 ms (test5-harness.log:8 to :10), so the range is 1028-1390 ms, not 'approximately 1.3 s'.\n\n### 3. report.md:286-302 - the one-paragraph conclusion omits the stdio-only scope limit, and section 6 recommends the untested alternative\nSection 3 (report.md:91) correctly states transport is stdio for both hops. The conclusion does not restate it. It says register_from_mcp 'works against the lazy proxy with no changes to ToolRegistry itself' and names two caveats (laziness, latency) - neither is transport scope. register_from_mcp supports http/sse/streamable-http/ws (verified: toolregistry/integrations/mcp/client.py:66-89); none was exercised. This matters concretely because report.md:267-268 proposes 'a longer-lived transport than stdio' as the remedy for cold-start - i.e. the document recommends a configuration for which it holds zero evidence, in the same section. Fix: add stdio-only to the caveat list in section 7.\n\n### 4. report.md:179 and proxy.py:16-17 - the capabilities claim is factually incorrect and is backed by no artifact\nBoth state that the proxy server capabilities 'omit tools/list_changed' / 'does NOT advertise tools/list_changed'. Source says otherwise: mcp/server/lowlevel/server.py:457-458 registers a tools/list handler whenever on_list_tools is passed (proxy.py:252 does), and server.py:605-606 then constructs types.ToolsCapability(list_changed=tools_changed) unconditionally for that case. tools_changed resolves to False on both branches (server.py:586 legacy listen_served is False since the proxy registers no subscriptions/listen handler; server.py:589 modern branch takes NotificationOptions default False). ToolsCapability.list_changed is typed bool or None defaulting to None (mcp_types/_types.py:449), so an explicit False is serialised, not dropped.\n\nSo the proxy advertises tools with listChanged false - it explicitly declares non-support rather than omitting the field. Arguably *stronger* evidence for the thesis than the report claims, but the stated fact is wrong. Compounding this: **no artifact anywhere captures the initialize response**, so this is the one protocol-level claim in the document with zero supporting evidence. Verdicts are unaffected; the accuracy of an evidence document is not.\n\n### 5. report.md:85-88 - the reproduce block cannot be trusted to reproduce\nSection 2 opens with 'Behavior varies by version, so the exact installed versions are recorded' (report.md:45), then section 3 gives an unpinned install: pip install 'toolregistry[mcp]'. That extra declares mcp<3,>=1.24.0 (verified in toolregistry-0.15.0.dist-info/METADATA:26, exactly as report.md:49 states). The committed artifacts depend on mcp 2.0-era APIs: the top-level mcp_types distribution (proxy.py:63), the Server(on_list_tools=..., on_call_tool=...) constructor form (proxy.py:249-254, backend.py:76-81), and add_notification_handler (proxy.py:255). A resolution anywhere in the permitted 1.24-2.x range that predates those APIs yields a non-functioning harness. Fix: pin toolregistry==0.15.0 and mcp==2.0.0 in the reproduce command.\n\n---\n\n## SHOULD CONSIDER (non-blocking)\n\n- **tests/phase123.py:97-105 vs report.md:120** - the report says the schema dump is 'identical to manifest.json'. The encoded check is a substring test for the literal add and nothing more; it compares no structure. It also does not feed test1_pass (phase123.py:107-113). I manually compared logs/tests1-3-harness.log:13-38 against manifest.json:12-28 and **the equality does hold** (properties a/b type number with matching descriptions, required [a,b], type object), so the verdict stands - but the harness would have passed on a divergent schema. Either strengthen the assertion or downgrade the prose to 'inspected and matching'.\n- **report.md:294 - the 'no richer proxy required' claim is scoped by an accident of the fixture.** manifest.json:30 sets output_schema null. mcp/client/session.py:1096-1100 shows that when a listed tool *does* declare an output schema, call_tool raises RuntimeError if the result carries no conforming structured content - and in a lazy-proxy design that schema comes from the **cached manifest** while the content comes from the **live backend**. Manifest-to-backend schema drift is therefore a hard-failure mode intrinsic to the identifier-first proposal, and it was never exercised. This belongs in section 6; it is arguably the single most consequential unanticipated behaviour available from this source read.\n- **report.md:182-197 - Test 5 Part B has no artifact.** The brief required a source inspection rather than inference, and one was genuinely performed - but its output exists only as report prose; logs/ holds nothing for it. I independently re-ran all of it and **every claim holds**: zero list_changed or listChanged matches across the whole toolregistry package, ClientSession referenced only in integrations/mcp/client.py, mcp/client/session.py:1289 send_roots_list_changed is roots-only, mcp/client/client.py:691 listen is on the unused high-level class. Recommend committing the grep transcript as logs/test5-source-read.log so the determinative evidence is reproducible without a reviewer rebuilding the venv.\n- **report.md:212 - the registration comparison sits inside noise.** The table places lazy proxy 2807 against direct backend 2786, a 21 ms gap. Derived within-condition spread for lazy-proxy registration alone is 214 ms: 2807 (tests1-3-harness.log:3 to :9), 2593 (test4-harness.log:2 to :8), 2665 (test5-harness.log:2 to :8). The between-condition difference is an order of magnitude below the within-condition variance; presented side by side it invites a precision the data does not carry.\n- **report.md:136-141 - the Test 2 decomposition does not close.** 1342 + 16 + 1255.5 + 10 = 2623.5 against a stated end-to-end of 2639, leaving 15.5 ms unattributed. The residual is the post-response tools/list at 1785983155864 (tests1-3-proxy.log:14) plus the return path - which the report itself documents as section 6 item 2 but never connects to the decomposition. Adding the residual line would make the breakdown auditable.\n- **report.md:157-172 - Test 4 largely validates the instrument, not the subject.** The respawn is performed by proxy.py:175-192, code written for this task; ToolRegistry never observes the failure (correctly noted at report.md:257-258). Test 4 is good evidence that a lazy proxy *can* self-heal, and weak evidence about ToolRegistry. Worth saying plainly, since section 7 lists it among the grounds for the register_from_mcp conclusion.\n- **report.md:251-253** - the section 6 item 3 supporting observation ('initial smoke test with an inherited PROXY_LOG_FILE produced no proxy log file') has no artifact in logs/. The underlying code claim is correct and I verified it (mcp/client/stdio.py:128, env=get_default_environment() or server.env), but the observation as written is unreproducible.\n- **report.md:1-19 - consumed_by is missing** from the front matter. It appears in eight other canonical docs in this repo (docs/standards/*, docs/research/*, docs/SESSION-END.md) and in the template at docs/standards/Documentation Standard.md:74. Everything else conforms: program EDASES, layer Research, document_type Research Finding, status Validated, authority Experimental are all permitted values per that standard at lines 112, 124, 138, 164, 179. Note the report uses last_updated while the standard template says last_update at line 86 - the report follows the repo-wide convention, so the **standard is the stale artifact here**, not the report.\n- **Observation/finding boundary leaks in a few table rows.** Section 1 sets the convention well and section 4 mostly honours it, but report.md:149 ('The proxy hop adds roughly 5-10 ms per call') is an inference sitting in an Observation column, and report.md:179 ('zero notification-send code paths') is a code fact rather than a runtime observation.\n\n---\n\n## NITS\n\n- report.md:250 cites mcp/client/stdio.py:75 for the env filtering; :75 is the function def, the inherited-var list is at :56 and the actual merge at :128. Substance correct, pointer imprecise.\n- manifest.json:4 embeds an absolute worktree path that will not exist after merge.\n- report.md:317-320 says the backend pid files are 'removed after the run'; the code only unlinks them at the *start* of the next run (phase123.py:53-54, phase4.py:66-67, phase5.py:44-45), so their absence reflects manual cleanup.\n- report.md:186-187 hedges the list_changed grep with 'excluding the vendored _vendor/ directory'. I grepped the whole package including _vendor: still zero matches. The claim is stronger than stated.\n- tests/phase4.py:161 - the exit code keys on test4_pass only; steady_ok (line 153) is logged but never gates the phase. Passed here, so no impact.\n\n---\n\n## NOTABLE POSITIVES\n\n- **Every source-line citation is exact.** I re-checked all of them against the installed packages: registration.py:90 (register_from_mcp def), tool_registry.py:185 (__str__, correct for print()), connection.py:106 (the reconnect-and-retry except), client.py:13, session.py:1088 (the list_tools call on output-schema cache miss - precisely right), session.py:1289, mcp client.py:691, plus the METADATA extra string mcp<3,>=1.24.0 and httpx>=0.28.1. Citation discipline of this quality is rare and made the review tractable.\n- **Versions are machine-derived, not transcribed.** common.py:51-65 pulls them via importlib.metadata into line 1 of every harness log, so the version record is itself evidence rather than an assertion.\n- **The Test 4 / Test 1 conflation the brief warned about did not occur.** The deviation is disclosed twice and in advance (report.md:95-106 and phase4.py:13-20), the reasoning is correct (persistent connection is lazy - verified at connection.py:42 and 82-85, with list_tools using a throwaway client at connection.py:79), the wording is precise ('impossible without a prior call or a non-lazy registry'), and report.md:168-170 explicitly separates the respawn path from the cold-start path. This is exactly right.\n- **Test 1's absence-of-contact evidence is doubly sound.** proxy.py:133-135 logs backend_spawn_start *before* creating the subprocess, so absence of the line is genuine evidence of absence of a spawn - and it is corroborated independently by backend_pid_file_exists=False (tests1-3-harness.log:10). Two independent signals for a negative claim.\n- **Test 3's pass criteria are genuinely strong** (phase123.py:175-180): spawn count, up count, forward count and the cardinality of the distinct-instance set are all asserted, not just the happy path.\n- **Section 6 is present, substantive and accurate.** I verified each item against both logs and source; the two-proxy-process discovery (item 1), the post-call tools/list refresh (item 2, reproduced in all three phases at tests1-3-proxy.log:14, test4-proxy.log:14, test5-proxy.log:14), the env filtering (item 3) and ToolRegistry's own retry layer (item 4) are all correct and non-obvious. Item 4 in particular is a real find.\n- **All four proxy behaviour claims verified in code, not prose:** spawn-on-first-call (ensure_ready reachable only from on_call_tool, proxy.py:213-228), cached tools/list (proxy.py:209-211, no backend reference), single reused session (proxy.py:119-120 early return), ms-stamped stderr (proxy.py:72-86). Grepping proxy.py for send_notification/notify returns only docstring text - the never-sends-notifications claim is sound at code level.\n- Hygiene: __pycache__ gitignored, venv excluded, harness logs carry the full interleaved stream (I confirmed all 24 proxy lines appear in tests1-3-harness.log).\n\n---\n\n## Summary of evidence vs recommendation\n\n**Evidence:** the five PASS verdicts follow from the logs; all stated arithmetic reconciles; all source citations are exact; the Test 4 methodology concern in the brief is fully addressed; the Test 5 source inspection was really performed and its conclusions independently replicate.\n\n**Recommendation (for the human orchestrator, not automatic direction):** the deliverable answers its question affirmatively and should be accepted *after* the five MUST FIX items are addressed - principally (a) re-derive the cold-start numbers as ranges over the three samples already in the corpus rather than single values, and (b) name stdio-only in the conclusion. The output-schema drift hazard under SHOULD CONSIDER is the finding most worth propagating into the identifier-first proposal itself, since it is a hard-failure mode intrinsic to serving a cached manifest for a live backend.","created_at":"2026-08-06T02:59:27.304420596Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:dqJJxDfhYp2s0PbFtktYlMdwFUCtHuPimgtdoZJSG+g","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgi3Y7k6zLHSnYcebt0U8E4gaHZmVqfalyr69g11qwxm8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAh62Rdl278kOUW53rk0OTAR8osJH/UMndcd5AJlsyEvLJHX3O6GciZ0qoV6l+BOBMqRcsh+KvgqgODOcpURnABg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:dqJJxDfhYp2s0PbFtktYlMdwFUCtHuPimgtdoZJSG+g","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgi3Y7k6zLHSnYcebt0U8E4gaHZmVqfalyr69g11qwxm8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJFKJk6A6gjV89xz8jL3dfX7JiwmYuiEHU4eZQN2qT7VPdQNIG+BRHua13+2PwdhxvNCd/U9fpdkJufPT+/o7Qw="}
